title: 'Audit P0 landed: the cardinal sin, an RCE, and a design decision that unified two findings'
summary: |
  Closed the first tranche of the soundness audit. Migrated the live fabric to refs mode (the single highest-value action — it re-engaged a CAS that was entirely disengaged on the founder's own data), then landed C-2 (a merge that silently dropped non-ASCII filename changes and sealed CLEAN), C-8 (git read failures read as "absent", and absent means DELETE), C-3 (an RCE: `.GIT` bypassed a case-sensitive guard and wrote an executable git hook), and C-4+C-12. The last two turned out to be one mechanism, discovered only because the builder implemented the fix, found it broke a legitimate tested recovery verb, and reverted rather than improvise. Founder ratified TD-2026-08-01-893: `stake recover` now seals an explicit reversion strand. 749 passing with ZERO skipped, up from 744 with 5 skipped.</summary>
  <body>## What actually produced the value

  Three things, none of which was writing code.

  **Ruling the test suite inadmissible.** Carried over from the instrument panel earlier the same day. It is why six critical findings surfaced in one pass after four prior reviews had accepted "tests pass."

  **Migrating before fixing.** `warpline refs migrate` was one existing command the CLI was already advertising, and it closed the only defect that could corrupt real data — the repo was in legacy selvage mode, which disengaged the pickId ref CAS entirely and left a stateId CAS structurally blind to 11 of 64 strands. No code was written for the highest-value action in the batch.

  **The builder refusing to improvise.** Kit implemented C-4, went green, discovered the fix broke `test/stake.test.ts:256` — a real tested disaster-recovery behaviour — and REVERTED, wrote the fixtures, proved the red, and skipped the blocks with the reasoning documented in the test header. That surfaced what six auditors had missed: C-4 and C-12 are one mechanism. What the audit filed as "stake recover leaves the fabric reporting corrupt, then self-heals on the next seal" IS the laundering, seen from the other end.

  ## The decision

  Root cause was structural: the v2 linear chain cannot represent the fork `recover` creates, and the live fabric has zero v3 strands, so the DAG that could represent it was unavailable. Three resolutions existed; the founder chose the reversion strand (TD-2026-08-01-893).

  The reason it is the right one goes beyond the bug: git records a reset in the reflog. A source-control system should not roll history back invisibly. The change makes Warpline's recovery MORE auditable than the thing it is measured against — what was reverted, from where, to where, by whom, why — and since `recover` is HUMAN_ONLY that is a real accountability record rather than a null field. It also preserves both invariants the audit found most load-bearing (append-only, linear v2 chain) instead of trading a known defect for a subtler one.

  ## The discriminator that mattered

  C-8's fix was the one where a wrong implementation would have been worse than the bug — turning legitimate absences into hard failures breaks every add/delete merge. The chosen discriminator is structural rather than error-parsing: presence now comes from the TREE ENTRY, because `ls-tree` exits 0 with empty output for a genuine absence. Any exit-code-based check would have been wrong, since `git show` exits 128 for both "not in this tree" and "corrupt pack."

  ## Two process notes worth carrying

  **The skipped count is the metric, not the passing count.** 744 passing / 5 skipped → 749 passing / 0 skipped. A suite that stays green because tests are skipped is the exact pattern the audit exists to kill, and it nearly recurred inside the audit's own remediation.

  **The builder truncated mid-report three times** across this batch, so the red-first confirmations for C-2/C-8/C-3 never arrived. I verified outcomes and the discriminator directly, but cannot attest those three tests were observed failing first. Long multi-fix agent runs are unreliable at this length; smaller scopes would trade wall-clock for evidential completeness.

  **Stale user-facing strings survive fixes.** Kit updated `stake.ts`'s module docs but left `cli.ts:1123` saying recover does "never a new strand" — false once it seals one. On this product that is not cosmetic: CLI descriptions are teaching surface, and a description contradicting its implementation is the same drift class as D-3.</body>
